    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Arabic, first recorded in inscriptions of the Arabian peninsula and its desert borderlands, became a world language with the Qur’an and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquests</a> of the 7th century.</li><li>As the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> it carried philosophy, science, medicine and mathematics, translated from Greek, Persian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and developed by scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and its literature is one of the richest in the world.</li><li>Today spoken by some 400 million people in a family of varieties, with a shared standard written language, it is the language of prayer for Muslims everywhere and has lent words from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> to the languages of the world.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>English words such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, alcohol, sugar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and zero come from Arabic, and algorithm comes from the name of the 9th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> mathematician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

